dict(pas="12.12", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^片=piàn/half (a) | 言/word | 可/can | 以/(by it) | 折=zhé/decide | 獄=yù/a lawsuit | 者，/the one who | ^其/surely | 由/Yóu | 也/(pause) | 與？」=yú/(is it not?)",
  l="The Master said: “One who with half a word can decide a lawsuit —\nsurely it is Yóu, is it not?”",
  i="The Master said: “If there is anyone who can settle a case on half a word, it must be Yóu.”",
  n="片言 is 'half a word' on Zhu Xi's reading (片言，半言), and 折 zhé (Zhu Xi: 之舌反) is 'to break', hence 'to decide' (折，斷也); 獄 is a lawsuit or criminal case. 可以 'can (by means of it)'; 者 closes the phrase into 'the one who…'. 其…也與 is the tentative 'surely it is…, is it not?', with 與 the question particle yú (Zhu Xi: 平聲). 由 is Zilu's personal name, written Yóu as in Book 11. Zhu Xi: Zilu was wholehearted and of good faith, clear and decisive, so that when he spoke men believed him and accepted it without waiting for him to finish. He Yan's Kong Anguo reads 片 as 偏 'one side': hearing a suit needs the statements of both parties to settle right and wrong; to decide a case trusting one side's words — only Zilu could do that."),
dict(w="子路=zǐ lù/Zilu | 無/had no | 宿=sù/kept overnight | 諾。/promise",
  l="Zilu had no promise kept overnight.",
  i="Zilu never let a promise wait till the next day.",
  n="宿 sù is 'to stay the night', hence 'to keep over, hold back'; 諾 is the word of assent, 'yes', and so a promise. Zhu Xi: 宿 is 留 'to keep', as in 宿怨 'a grudge one keeps'; Zilu was eager to make good his word and never let a promise lie. He prints the line in one chapter with the Master's saying before it: the recorders, he says, set it down there to show that Zilu won men's trust because he had trained himself to it all along. He quotes Yin Tun's example: a man of the small state of Little Zhu (小邾射) fled to Lu with the town of 句繹 and said, 'If Ji Lu makes the compact with me, I need no oath' — a state of a thousand chariots would not trust its own oath, yet trusted one word of Zilu's; one word could settle a case because the good faith came before the words, and he kept no promise waiting so as to keep that faith whole. He Yan's commentary reads 宿 another way, as 'beforehand' (宿猶豫也): Zilu held so firmly to his word that, fearing things might change when the time came, he made no promises in advance."),
# ---------------- 12.13 ----------------
dict(pas="12.13", w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^聽/hearing | 訟，/lawsuits | ^吾/I | 猶/am like | 人/others | 也。/(assertion) | ^必/if (it) must (be anything) | 也/(pause) | 使/making | 無/(there be) no | 訟/lawsuits | 乎！」/(surely: 'would it not be')",
  l="The Master said: “Hearing lawsuits,\nI am like others.\nIf it must be anything, it would be making there be no lawsuits!”",
  i="The Master said: “At hearing cases I do as anyone does. What I would want is that there be no cases to hear.”",
  n="聽訟 'to listen to suits', that is, to try cases. 猶人 'like others'. 必也…乎 is the shape of 3.7 (必也射乎): 'if it must be something, it would be…'. 使無訟 'to cause there to be no suits': 使 'to make (it so that)'. Zhu Xi quotes Fan Zuyu: hearing suits deals with the branches and dams the flow; set the root right and clear the source, and there will be no suits. And Yang Shi: Zilu could decide a case with half a word, but did not know how to govern a state by the rites and deference, and so could not bring the people to have no suits; the recorders set this saying after the last to show that the sage did not count hearing suits hard, but valued making the people have none. In He Yan, Bao Xian (苞氏): 'I am the same as others'; Wang Su: to have no suits is to transform the people beforehand."),
