dict(pas="12.17", w="季康子=jì kāng zǐ/Ji Kangzi | 問/asked about | 政/government | 於/of | 孔子。/Kongzi | ^孔子/Kongzi | 對/answered | 曰：「/saying | ^政/government | 者，/(as for) | 正/setting right | 也。/(is) | ^子/you, sir | 帥=shuài/lead | 以/by | 正，/being right | ^孰/who | 敢/would dare | 不/not (to be) | 正？」/right",
  l="Ji Kangzi asked Kongzi about government.\nKongzi answered, saying: “Government is setting right.\nYou, sir, lead by being right —\nwho would dare not be right?”",
  i="Ji Kangzi asked Kongzi about governing. Kongzi answered: “To govern is to put straight. Go before them straight yourself, sir, and who will dare to be crooked?”",
  n="政 zhèng 'government' is written with 正 zhèng 'straight, right' and sounds the same: the answer is a definition by pun, in the frame X者Y也 'X is Y'. 子 is 'you, sir', polite address to a grandee; 帥 shuài 'to lead'; 孰敢 'who would dare'. Ji Kangzi is the head of the Ji house (2.20). Zhu Xi quotes Fan Zuyu: no one who was not upright himself has ever set others upright. And Hu Yin: from the middle of its history the government of Lu had been in the grandees' hands, and their household retainers copied them, seizing towns and rising in revolt — unrightness at its worst; so Kongzi told him this, hoping Kangzi would master himself by uprightness and undo the old ways of the three houses — sadly Kangzi was sunk in desire for gain and could not. He Yan's Zheng Xuan: Ji Kangzi was the senior minister of Lu, the leader (帥) of all its ministers. Variant: He Yan's text reads 子帥而正 'if you lead and are right'."),
